VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-018
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe systemic exhaustion, sudden cognitive decline, and suspected myxedema crisis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an acute inability to stand due to profound lethargy, severe brain fog, and subjective feeling of extreme cold. Urgent physical evaluation was required to rule out myxedema coma, acute adrenal insufficiency, and to objectively assess hemodynamic stability prior to morning hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I feel like I am moving through wet cement, I am freezing to the bone, and I can't form a complete thought.
History of Present Illness: Patient with known Hashimoto's reports a rapid, 3-day decline into profound systemic fatigue. Although recent bloodwork reportedly showed a normal TSH, the patient is experiencing symptoms driven by high TPO antibodies and systemic inflammation. Symptoms include crushing physical lethargy, severe brain fog resembling early dementia, and cold intolerance. Patient notes they could not muster the energy to prepare food for the past 24 hours.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to extreme physical exhaustion.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; inadequate caloric and fluid intake due to fatigue.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/70 mmHg | HR: 60 bpm | Temp: 36.4 C | RR: 14 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Puffy facial appearance, visibly exhausted, wearing multiple layers of heavy clothing. Speech is slow.
Physical Exam: Thyroid is mildly enlarged, firm, and non-tender on palpation. Skin is dry and cool to the touch. Deep tendon reflexes exhibit a delayed relaxation phase. Cardiovascular exam reveals mild bradycardia but normal rhythm and adequate peripheral perfusion. Presentation is classic for autoimmune thyroiditis with euthyroid fatigue driven by systemic inflammation.
Environmental Observations: House is heavily heated. Patient is wrapped in multiple heavy blankets.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking regular Thyroxine as prescribed. No missed doses.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hashimoto's Thyroiditis Inflammatory Phenotype - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Myxedema coma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient is alert and conversational, normotensive, and maintaining safe (though low-normal) body temperature and respiratory drive.

Acute adrenal insufficiency (Addisonian crisis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of severe hypotension, intractable vomiting, and normal skin pigmentation.

Major Depressive Disorder with catatonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear autoimmune physical signs (delayed reflexes, goiter, cold skin) and desire to improve function despite physical inability.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of profound lethargy, bradycardia, and cognitive slowing warranted an urgent in-home assessment to explicitly rule out life-threatening myxedema coma, which requires emergent intravenous endocrine rescue. After confirming hemodynamic stability and adequate respiratory effort, it was determined the patient is suffering from an acute inflammatory autoimmune flare rather than absolute hormone failure. Medical reassurance and stabilization were necessary to prevent a highly distressing and unnecessary emergency transport.

